<div class="page-wrap prose">
  <section id="start" aria-labelledby="start-title">
    <h2 id="start-title">The question behind this page</h2>
    <p>
      A user asks you to delete their data. The row is removed from the source
      database, the connector emits a delete event, and the event reaches the
      warehouse. <strong>Is the data gone?</strong> Usually not. The delete
      event changed one copy of the data. Every other copy lives under its own
      setting and its own schedule, and some of those schedules are measured in
      weeks.
    </p>
    <p>
      The same mistake runs the other way too. A sink that handles a delete by
      removing the row can bring the row back later, because delivery is
      at-least-once and an older change can arrive after a newer one.
      Both problems have one answer: a delete is an event with a position,
      applied by an idempotent sink, and every other copy has a known expiry.
    </p>
    <p class="callout">
      <strong>This is not legal advice.</strong> This page explains the
      technical side: where data can still exist and which setting governs it.
      Whether a particular copy must be erased, by when, and whether an
      exception applies is a legal decision for your lawyer or data protection
      officer. Nothing here promises that following it satisfies any regulation.
    </p>
    <p>
      It extends <a href="{{ '/security/' | url }}#erasure">Deletion requests
      and an append-only log</a> and does not repeat its three strategies
      (never carry the identifier in the clear, compaction plus tombstones,
      crypto-shredding). It assumes the site&rsquo;s standing rule: delivery is
      at-least-once, and the sink is idempotent, keyed on the primary key and
      ordered by the <strong>source log position</strong>
      (<a href="{{ '/glossary/#lsn-scn' | url }}">LSN or SCN</a>), never by a
      timestamp or a Kafka offset. End-to-end exactly-once across independent
      systems is not achievable; see
      <a href="{{ '/exactly-once/' | url }}">Exactly-Once Semantics</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="three-deletes" aria-labelledby="three-deletes-title">
    <h2 id="three-deletes-title">Three things people call a delete</h2>
    <p>
      A CDC pipeline has three different objects that all get called
      &ldquo;the delete&rdquo;. They are made by different components, for
      different readers, and they do different jobs.
    </p>
    <table>
      <caption class="sr-only">The three kinds of delete in a CDC pipeline</caption>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col"></th>
          <th scope="col">Source DELETE event</th>
          <th scope="col">Kafka tombstone</th>
          <th scope="col">Sink delete marker</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">What it is</th>
          <td>An ordinary change event with <code>op: "d"</code>, the row as it was (the before image) and the <code>source</code> block.</td>
          <td>A Kafka record with the row&rsquo;s key and a <code>null</code> value.</td>
          <td>A row in the target table with a deleted flag and the position of the delete.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Made by</th>
          <td>The connector, from the database log.</td>
          <td>The connector, after the delete event. Debezium&rsquo;s PostgreSQL connector does this when <code>tombstones.on.delete</code> is <code>true</code>, which is the default; check the setting for your connector.</td>
          <td>Your sink&rsquo;s upsert or <code>MERGE</code>.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">What it is for</th>
          <td>Telling consumers that the row was deleted, and when in the log.</td>
          <td>Telling Kafka&rsquo;s log cleaner that it may drop the key&rsquo;s older records on a compacted topic, and later the tombstone itself.</td>
          <td>Hiding the row from readers and stopping an older, replayed change from re-creating it.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Carries a position?</th>
          <td>Yes, in the <code>source</code> block.</td>
          <td><strong>No.</strong> It has a key and no value; there is nothing in it to compare.</td>
          <td>Yes, by design. That is the point of keeping it.</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p class="muted small">
      Sources: Kafka design documentation, &ldquo;Log Compaction Basics&rdquo;;
      Debezium PostgreSQL connector, &ldquo;Tombstone events&rdquo; and
      <code>tombstones.on.delete</code>. Glossary:
      <a href="{{ '/glossary/#tombstone' | url }}">tombstone</a>,
      <a href="{{ '/glossary/#compaction' | url }}">compaction</a>.
    </p>

    <h3 id="tombstone-not-delete">Why a tombstone alone does not delete a warehouse row</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>It is addressed to Kafka, not to your table.</strong> Kafka&rsquo;s
        documentation describes a key with a null payload as a delete <em>from
        the log</em>. Whether anything downstream deletes a row on seeing it is
        up to the sink.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Sinks differ and often default to not deleting.</strong>
        Debezium&rsquo;s own JDBC sink, by default, does not delete the row in
        the target when it consumes a delete or a tombstone; you must set
        <code>delete.enabled=true</code> and give it a primary key mapping.
        Other connectors differ. Read yours.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>A flattened stream may carry only the tombstone.</strong> The
        Debezium event-flattening transform drops the delete event and keeps
        the tombstone by default. The sink then sees a key and nothing else:
        no operation code, no position, no before image. To keep a delete
        as a normal record, set the transform&rsquo;s delete handling to
        <code>rewrite</code> (or <code>rewrite-with-tombstone</code>) and add
        the position with <code>add.fields</code> (for example
        <code>op,table,lsn</code>). The option name has changed between
        Debezium versions; check yours.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Acting on the tombstone is still a physical delete.</strong>
        A sink that removes the row when the tombstone arrives has the problem
        in the next section.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>None of this touches earlier events.</strong> The inserts and
        updates that carried the person&rsquo;s values are separate records in
        the topic, and in every copy made from it.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section id="resurrection" aria-labelledby="resurrection-title">
    <h2 id="resurrection-title">Resurrection: the late, older update</h2>
    <p>
      Delivery is at-least-once, so an event can arrive twice and an older one
      can arrive after a newer one. Common ways that happens: a connector restart
      re-emits changes from before the last committed offset, a consumer is
      rewound, a failed update waits in a
      <a href="{{ '/dlq-triage/' | url }}">dead-letter queue</a> and is
      re-driven later, or a second pipeline writes the same table. The row
      with <code>id = 7</code> sees these changes, at these source log
      positions:
    </p>
    <ol>
      <li>Position 100: insert, email <code>a@example.com</code>.</li>
      <li>Position 140: update, email <code>b@example.com</code>.</li>
      <li>Position 180: delete.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      Then the update at 140 is delivered again, after the delete. Two sinks
      handle the same arrivals:
    </p>
    <table>
      <caption class="sr-only">State of one key after each arrival, with a physical delete and with a delete marker</caption>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Arrival</th>
          <th scope="col">Sink A: removes the row on delete</th>
          <th scope="col">Sink B: delete marker with position</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">1. insert at 100</th>
          <td><code>(7, a@, 100)</code></td>
          <td><code>(7, a@, deleted = false, 100)</code></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">2. update at 140</th>
          <td><code>(7, b@, 140)</code></td>
          <td><code>(7, b@, deleted = false, 140)</code></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">3. delete at 180</th>
          <td>row removed</td>
          <td><code>(7, NULL, deleted = true, 180)</code></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">4. update at 140 again</th>
          <td><strong>the row is back:</strong> <code>(7, b@, 140)</code>, with the email that was supposed to be erased</td>
          <td>unchanged; the guard asks whether 180 &lt; 140 and it is not</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">5. delete at 180 again</th>
          <td>row removed again</td>
          <td>unchanged; 180 &lt; 180 is false, so applying it twice is harmless</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
      Sink A removed the stored position along with the row, so the late update
      found nothing to compare against, took the insert path and re-created the
      row. Sink B kept the delete as a row that carries its position and nulls
      the payload, so the same guard that handles every other replay handles
      this one. This is the rule from
      <a href="{{ '/partitioning/' | url }}#recon">Making Sinks Bulletproof</a>
      applied to deletes. A tombstone cannot do this job: it has no position.
    </p>
    <pre><code class="language-sql">CREATE TABLE customers (
  id         BIGINT  PRIMARY KEY,
  email      TEXT,                          -- NULL on a delete: erase the payload with the marker
  is_deleted BOOLEAN NOT NULL DEFAULT FALSE,
  source_lsn BIGINT  NOT NULL               -- position of the change that wrote this row
);

-- Apply ONE change (a batch is first reduced to the newest change per key,
-- as in the merge cookbook). A delete is the same statement with
-- email = NULL and is_deleted = TRUE; it also inserts a marker for a key
-- the table has never seen, so a late older insert cannot create the row.
INSERT INTO customers (id, email, is_deleted, source_lsn)
VALUES (7, 'b@example.com', FALSE, 140)
ON CONFLICT (id) DO UPDATE
SET email      = EXCLUDED.email,
    is_deleted = EXCLUDED.is_deleted,
    source_lsn = EXCLUDED.source_lsn
WHERE customers.source_lsn &lt; EXCLUDED.source_lsn;

-- Readers never see markers.
CREATE VIEW customers_current AS
SELECT id, email FROM customers WHERE NOT is_deleted;</code></pre>
    <p class="muted small">
      Tested as written against SQLite in this site&rsquo;s unit tests (the
      five arrivals above, and the case where the delete arrives first). Not run
      against PostgreSQL, whose <code>ON CONFLICT ... DO UPDATE ... WHERE</code>
      form it follows. The position is assumed to be one number that is unique
      per change for a key; if it can repeat, compare a (position, ordinal)
      pair as described on the page linked above. The full per-engine versions
      are in the <a href="{{ '/merge-cookbook/' | url }}">merge cookbook</a>,
      and the pattern is explained on
      <a href="{{ '/materialization/' | url }}#merge">Materialization 101</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="trace" aria-labelledby="trace-title">
    <h2 id="trace-title">Trace one deleted row</h2>
    <p>
      Take one person&rsquo;s row and list every place a copy can exist after
      the delete event has been applied in the sink. All values below were read
      from the vendors&rsquo; documentation on 2026-10-09 (versions in
      <a href="#limits">What this page did not test</a>); defaults change, so
      check the number for your version before you rely on it.
    </p>

    <h3 id="trace-stream">In the stream</h3>
    <table>
      <caption class="sr-only">Copies of a deleted row in the Kafka stream and what governs them</caption>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Copy</th>
          <th scope="col">Does the delete event touch it?</th>
          <th scope="col">What governs how long it lives</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Earlier change events in the topic (the values)</th>
          <td>No. They are separate records.</td>
          <td>
            <code>cleanup.policy</code> (default <code>delete</code>) and, for
            that policy, <code>retention.ms</code> (default 7 days). With
            <code>compact</code>, older records for the key are removed when
            the log cleaner runs; the newest segment is never compacted, and
            <code>min.compaction.lag.ms</code> and
            <code>max.compaction.lag.ms</code> bound when a record becomes
            eligible. Compaction is background work, not a deadline.
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">The delete event itself</th>
          <td>It is the delete event. It holds the before image.</td>
          <td>
            With <code>tombstones.on.delete=false</code> only the delete event
            is emitted, and Debezium&rsquo;s documentation says that Kafka can
            remove all messages for a key only when the last one has a null
            value. On a compacted topic that leaves the delete event, before
            image included, as the key&rsquo;s last record.
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">The tombstone</th>
          <td>It is the delete signal for the log.</td>
          <td>
            <code>delete.retention.ms</code> (default 1 day). A consumer that is
            more than this far behind can miss it. See below.
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Dead-letter topic</th>
          <td>No. An event that failed earlier may be sitting here with the values.</td>
          <td>
            A normal topic with its own <code>retention.ms</code> and
            <code>cleanup.policy</code>, named by
            <code>errors.deadletterqueue.topic.name</code> on a sink connector
            (blank by default). With
            <code>errors.deadletterqueue.context.headers.enable</code> it also
            carries error context headers. See
            <a href="{{ '/dlq-triage/' | url }}">DLQ triage</a> and
            <a href="{{ '/glossary/#dead-letter-queue' | url }}">the glossary</a>.
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Archive or raw staging table</th>
          <td>No. Append-only copies keep every version.</td>
          <td>
            Whatever lifecycle rule you set. The lessons here advise keeping raw
            envelopes for N days so you can reprocess
            (<a href="{{ '/materialization/' | url }}#late-arrivals">late arrivals</a>),
            and some pipelines archive the whole stream on purpose
            (<a href="{{ '/use-cases/' | url }}">use cases</a>). That N is also
            your erasure delay for this copy.
          </td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p class="muted small">
      Sources: Apache Kafka topic configuration and design (Log Compaction)
      pages; Kafka Connect configuration; Debezium PostgreSQL connector.
    </p>

    <h3 id="trace-sink">In the sink and after it</h3>
    <table>
      <caption class="sr-only">Copies of a deleted row in the sink, its history and backups, and what governs them</caption>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Copy</th>
          <th scope="col">What the delete does to it</th>
          <th scope="col">What governs how long it lives</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Current-state row</th>
          <td>With a marker: the payload is nulled and the row stays as <code>(key, deleted, position)</code>.</td>
          <td>Your merge, and your purge job (below).</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Delta Lake table history</th>
          <td><code>DELETE</code> removes the data from the latest version only; it stays in physical storage until old versions are vacuumed.</td>
          <td>
            <code>VACUUM</code> is never automatic; it removes unreferenced
            files older than <code>delta.deletedFileRetentionDuration</code>
            (default 7 days). Time travel is limited by
            <code>delta.logRetentionDuration</code> (default 30 days) <em>and</em>
            by whether the data files still exist. Change data feed files in
            <code>_change_data</code> follow the same retention and go with
            <code>VACUUM</code>.
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Iceberg table snapshots</th>
          <td>A delete is a new snapshot. Old snapshots, and the data files they reference, stay available for time travel.</td>
          <td>
            Snapshots accumulate until the <code>expireSnapshots</code>
            operation runs. Expiry uses
            <code>history.expire.max-snapshot-age-ms</code> (default 5 days) and
            <code>history.expire.min-snapshots-to-keep</code> (default 1); data
            files are removed only when no remaining snapshot references them.
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Iceberg delete files (format v2 and later)</th>
          <td>A row-level delete can be a position delete or equality delete file. The data files are immutable and are not rewritten.</td>
          <td>
            <code>write.delete.mode</code>, <code>write.update.mode</code> and
            <code>write.merge.mode</code> default to <code>copy-on-write</code>;
            <code>merge-on-read</code> writes delete files instead. The deleted
            values stay in the original data file until it is rewritten
            (compaction) and the older snapshots are expired.
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">BigQuery table with CDC</th>
          <td>A CDC <code>DELETE</code> counts as applied only once <code>upsert_stream_apply_watermark</code> has passed the time it was streamed.</td>
          <td>
            BigQuery keeps deletes for a 2-day retention window to handle
            out-of-order deletes. If CDC operations fail, or the reservation
            has too little capacity, the documentation warns that you might
            unintentionally retain data you meant to delete. Then time travel
            (7 days by default, 2 to 7 configurable) and a fixed 7-day
            fail-safe for the operations the page lists (table or partition
            deletion and replacement).
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Snowflake table</th>
          <td>Time Travel preserves the pre-delete state of the data.</td>
          <td>
            <code>DATA_RETENTION_TIME_IN_DAYS</code>: 1 day by default, and up
            to 90 days for permanent objects on Enterprise Edition or higher.
            Then a non-configurable 7-day Fail-safe that only Snowflake can
            recover from.
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Backups, replicas, exports, caches, search indexes</th>
          <td>Not touched. A restore brings the old rows back.</td>
          <td>Their own schedules. See the erasure section.</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p class="muted small">
      Sources: Delta Lake batch, update and utility documentation and change
      data feed documentation; Apache Iceberg maintenance, configuration and
      specification pages; BigQuery change data capture and time travel
      documentation; Snowflake Time Travel and Fail-safe documentation. URLs in
      <a href="#resources">Further resources</a>.
    </p>

    <h3 id="late-consumer">Kafka: what a consumer that falls behind misses</h3>
    <p>
      Kafka&rsquo;s documentation is specific. A consumer that reads from the
      start sees the final state of every key, and sees the delete markers too,
      <em>provided it reaches the head of the log in less than
      <code>delete.retention.ms</code></em>. Because the cleaner removes
      tombstones while consumers read, a consumer that lags by more than that
      can miss them. The setting also bounds how long a read that starts at
      offset 0 has to finish to get a valid snapshot of the final state.
    </p>
    <p>
      Two consequences for a CDC sink. First, a sink that already holds the
      row and then falls behind by more than a day (the default) can come back
      to a topic where the tombstone is gone and the log no longer says the key
      was deleted, so it keeps the row. Second, a sink bootstrapped by reading
      a compacted topic needs to finish within the window or it may end up with
      a state that never saw the delete. Alert when consumer lag approaches
      <code>delete.retention.ms</code> for any compacted topic you depend on
      for deletes. See
      <a href="{{ '/observability/' | url }}">observability</a> for lag signals.
    </p>

    <h3 id="two-clocks">Delta Lake: two clocks, and VACUUM safety</h3>
    <p>
      The 30-day figure is the time-travel history. The bytes leave storage on
      a different clock: the 7-day deleted-file retention, and only when
      someone runs <code>VACUUM</code>. Delta recommends keeping the retention
      interval at least 7 days because shorter intervals can break concurrent
      readers or writers, and has a safety check that blocks shorter intervals
      unless you turn it off. So you cannot speed up erasure without taking a
      reader-safety risk; that trade belongs in your written decision. A managed
      platform may schedule maintenance for you; check yours rather than assuming
      either way.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="purge" aria-labelledby="purge-title">
    <h2 id="purge-title">Purging markers safely</h2>
    <p>
      Markers cost storage and they hold a key (and a position) for a person
      who asked to be forgotten, so they need a retention policy. The wrong
      moment to remove one is when &ldquo;the row has been deleted for a
      while&rdquo;. A marker exists to defeat older events. It can go when
      both of these are true:
    </p>
    <ol>
      <li>
        <strong>The delete has propagated.</strong> Every consumer and replica
        of the table has applied or read the marker: downstream incremental
        models, marts, caches and search indexes. Purge it first and a
        downstream job that has not seen it yet never learns of the delete.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Nothing that can be replayed holds an older event for that
        key.</strong> The oldest source position that any replay source can
        still deliver must be newer than the marker. Replay sources are the
        topic (up to its retention, or for a compacted topic its
        un-compacted history), the dead-letter queue, the archive, the raw
        staging table, and any re-snapshot or restored backup you might load.
        Replays carry the position they had the first time, so this is a
        comparison of positions, not of clock times.
      </li>
    </ol>
    <pre><code class="language-sql">-- Physical delete (purge): the only DELETE in this design.
-- :purge_below is the LOWEST source position that any replay source (topic,
-- dead-letter queue, archive, staging, planned re-snapshot) can still deliver,
-- and it is only valid if every consumer of the table is already past the marker.
-- The comparison is strict: a marker AT the bound stays.
DELETE FROM customers
WHERE is_deleted AND source_lsn &lt; :purge_below;</code></pre>
    <p>
      This is the same purge as in the
      <a href="{{ '/merge-cookbook/' | url }}">merge cookbook</a>. Computing
      <code>:purge_below</code> is the hard part: read the first retained
      record of each partition of each replay source and take the lowest
      <code>source</code> position among them, within one source log lineage
      (positions restart after a failover that loses the log tail or a
      restore; see the lineage note on
      <a href="{{ '/partitioning/' | url }}#recon">Making Sinks Bulletproof</a>).
      If you cannot get positions out of a replay source, bound by the age of the
      marker against the <em>longest</em> replay window plus a margin, using
      load time, not commit time.
    </p>
    <p>
      Vendors that keep delete markers for you chose a window, not a proof.
      BigQuery holds CDC deletes for 2 days, and Databricks keeps a deleted
      row as a tombstone for 2 days by default
      (<code>pipelines.cdc.tombstoneGCThresholdInSeconds</code>), telling you to
      set it above the longest expected delay between an event arriving and the
      pipeline running. Treat those as the same question your own purge
      answers: how late can an older event be?
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="erasure" aria-labelledby="erasure-title">
    <h2 id="erasure-title">Erasure requests: the technical question</h2>
    <p class="callout">
      <strong>Not legal advice.</strong> Read the regulation yourself and ask a
      lawyer. This section only separates what the law text says from the
      engineering facts, so that you can bring the right facts to that
      conversation.
    </p>

    <h3 id="what-article-17-says">What the official text says</h3>
    <p>
      The text below is from Regulation (EU) 2016/679 as published on
      EUR-Lex (links in <a href="#resources">Further resources</a>).
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Article 17(1)</strong> gives the data subject the right to
        obtain erasure of personal data from the controller &ldquo;without
        undue delay&rdquo;, and obliges the controller to erase without undue
        delay, where one of six listed grounds applies (for example the data are
        no longer necessary for the purposes they were collected for, or the
        data subject withdraws consent and there is no other legal ground).
        &ldquo;Without undue delay&rdquo; is not a number of days.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Article 17(3)</strong> says paragraphs 1 and 2 do <em>not</em>
        apply to the extent that processing is necessary for: (a) exercising
        freedom of expression and information; (b) compliance with a legal
        obligation that requires the processing, or a task in the public
        interest or in the exercise of official authority; (c) public health
        reasons; (d) archiving in the public interest, scientific or historical
        research or statistical purposes, where erasure would likely make the
        objectives impossible or seriously impair them; or (e) the
        establishment, exercise or defence of legal claims.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Article 19</strong> requires the controller to communicate the
        erasure to each recipient to whom the data were disclosed, &ldquo;unless
        this proves impossible or involves disproportionate effort&rdquo;.
        Every downstream consumer of your topics is a candidate recipient.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Article 12(3)</strong> requires the controller to provide
        information on action taken on the request without undue delay and in
        any event within one month of receipt, extendable by two further months
        where necessary. That is the deadline for telling the person what was
        done. It is not a statement about how long each copy may take to
        expire.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      The 17(3) exceptions apply &ldquo;to the extent that&rdquo; processing is
      necessary, so they can cover some data and not other data. In pipeline
      terms that means a decision per table or per column, not per person:
      the invoice rows you must keep and the marketing profile you must erase
      can sit in the same topic. Your pipeline needs to be able to say which
      copy it kept and on what stated basis. Who decides, and what basis is
      acceptable, is for your lawyer.
    </p>
    <p>
      The text of the regulation, as published, does not use the words
      &ldquo;backup&rdquo;, &ldquo;snapshot&rdquo; or &ldquo;time travel&rdquo;,
      so it does not answer the engineering questions below. The UK
      Information Commissioner&rsquo;s Office, writing about the UK&rsquo;s own
      version of the regulation, has published guidance that data in backups
      should be put &ldquo;beyond use&rdquo; until overwritten on an
      established schedule, that individuals should be told clearly what will
      happen to backup copies, and that the outcome is context-specific. That
      is one regulator&rsquo;s published view, not EU law, and others may
      differ.
    </p>

    <h3 id="technical-questions">The technical questions</h3>
    <p>For each row of the trace tables above, answer these in writing:</p>
    <ol>
      <li>Does a copy of this person&rsquo;s data still exist there after the delete is applied?</li>
      <li>Until when, and which setting decides that date?</li>
      <li>Who or what can still read it (time-travel queries, a restore, a replay)?</li>
      <li>What would remove it sooner, and what does that cost (for Delta, a shorter retention has a reader-safety cost)?</li>
      <li>Is any exception under 17(3) being relied on for this copy, who decided that, and where is it recorded?</li>
    </ol>

    <h3 id="backups">Backups and retention copies</h3>
    <p>
      Backups, replicas, exports and archives are separate stores with their own
      schedules. A delete event does not remove anything from them, and neither
      does applying a marker in the sink. Time-travel windows and fail-safe
      periods behave like backups: you cannot edit them, you can only wait for
      them to expire (or shorten them going forward), and a table recovered from
      them contains the row again.
    </p>
    <p>
      The engineering consequence is a <strong>restore rule</strong>. After
      you restore a table from a backup or a time-travel version, the table is
      older than the deletes you have since applied, and the topic may no longer
      hold those deletes. Keep a record of erasures (a surrogate key and the
      position and date of the delete, kept for at least as long as your oldest
      restorable backup) and re-apply it after any restore, before readers see
      the table. Whether and how long you may keep that record is again for your
      lawyer.
    </p>

    <h3 id="shorten">What you can do to make the answers shorter</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>
        Do not put the identifier in the clear in the first place, or encrypt
        per subject and destroy the key
        (<a href="{{ '/security/' | url }}#erasure">the strategies on the
        Security page</a>). That works on every copy at once, including the
        ones you cannot edit.
      </li>
      <li>
        Choose retention on purpose for the history of tables that hold personal
        data (time travel, snapshot age, fail-safe where configurable), and
        schedule the job that makes expiry real (<code>VACUUM</code>,
        <code>expireSnapshots</code>).
      </li>
      <li>
        Null the payload columns in the same statement that writes the marker,
        and keep only the key and the position.
      </li>
      <li>
        Give the dead-letter topic, the archive and the staging tables a
        retention that you have written down, rather than the default.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section id="monday" aria-labelledby="monday-title">
    <h2 id="monday-title">What to do Monday</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>
        Pick one table that holds personal data and fill in the two trace tables
        for it, with the actual values from your cluster and warehouse, not the
        defaults on this page.
      </li>
      <li>
        Read your connector config: is <code>tombstones.on.delete</code> what
        you intend, and if you flatten events, does the sink still receive the
        delete with its position (<code>rewrite</code> plus
        <code>add.fields</code>) or only a bare tombstone?
      </li>
      <li>
        Find out what your sink does on a tombstone and on a delete event (for
        the Debezium JDBC sink, <code>delete.enabled</code>). If it removes the row,
        replay the five arrivals from the resurrection table against it.
      </li>
      <li>
        Make the sink keep delete markers that carry a position, and put a view
        in front so readers filter them. Null the payload on delete.
      </li>
      <li>
        Write down your replay window: topic retention, dead-letter retention,
        archive and staging retention, and the oldest backup you could restore.
        Compute <code>:purge_below</code> from it and schedule the purge.
      </li>
      <li>
        Add an alert for consumer lag against <code>delete.retention.ms</code>
        on every compacted topic that carries deletes.
      </li>
      <li>
        For Delta, Iceberg, BigQuery or Snowflake, check that the maintenance
        that makes expiry real is actually scheduled (<code>VACUUM</code>,
        <code>expireSnapshots</code>), and record the retention numbers.
      </li>
      <li>
        Set the retention of the dead-letter topic and the raw archive on
        purpose. If something is kept longer than a request deadline allows,
        write down why.
      </li>
      <li>
        Write the restore rule: after any restore, re-apply the erasure record
        before the table is readable.
      </li>
      <li>
        Take the filled-in tables to your lawyer or data protection officer and
        ask what is acceptable for each copy. Record which Article 17(3)
        exceptions, if any, they accept, and by whom.
      </li>
    </ol>
  </section>

  <section id="limits" aria-labelledby="limits-title">
    <h2 id="limits-title">What this page did not test</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        No vendor service was run. Every figure in the trace tables comes from
        the documentation pages listed below, read on 2026-10-09: Apache Kafka
        documentation at <code>kafka.apache.org/43/</code>, Debezium
        &ldquo;stable&rdquo; documentation, Delta Lake documentation at
        <code>docs.delta.io</code>, Apache Iceberg 1.12.0 documentation,
        BigQuery and Snowflake documentation, and the Databricks pipeline
        reference. Check your version.
      </li>
      <li>
        The SQL on this page was executed against SQLite only. It is written in
        the PostgreSQL <code>ON CONFLICT</code> form but was not run on
        PostgreSQL, and not on any warehouse.
      </li>
      <li>
        Not verified from vendor documentation: whether a managed Delta
        platform runs <code>VACUUM</code> for you; whether BigQuery&rsquo;s
        fail-safe covers row-level CDC deletes (its page lists table and
        partition deletion and replacement); how an Iceberg engine you use
        applies delete files during compaction.
      </li>
      <li>
        The legal text is quoted from EUR-Lex. How a regulator or court would
        read it for any pipeline is not covered, and the UK guidance is one
        regulator&rsquo;s view of a different instrument.
      </li>
      <li>
        The Playground&rsquo;s <em>Retention &amp; Erasure</em> scenario
        exists, but it does not model a late older update after a delete or any
        storage-level retention, so this page does not link to it as a demo.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

